I didn't change anything: all three file writes were blocked by permissions, so the code and tests are exactly as they were.

**What's wrong now:** `src/services/user-service.js` calls the old callback-style `query`/`update` functions directly. The project is moving to `UserRepository`, a promise-based class that already exists in `src/repositories/user-repository.js`.

**What I was going to change:**

1. **`src/services/user-service.js`**: drop the `query`/`update` import and route every function through the repository, returning promises:
   ```js
   import { UserRepository } from '../repositories/user-repository.js';

   const users = new UserRepository();

   export function listActiveUsers() { return users.findActive(); }
   export function findUser(id) { return users.findById(id); }
   export function softDeleteUser(id, when) { return users.softDelete(id, when); }
   ```
2. **`tests/user-service.test.js`**: make both tests `async` and `await` the functions instead of passing callbacks.
3. **`src/api/routes.js`**: the `/users` and `/users/:id` handlers still pass callbacks to these functions. Without a change they would never respond, even though the unit tests would pass. I'd wrap them, e.g. `listActiveUsers().then((r) => cb(null, r), cb)`.

If you allow writes to those three files, I'll make the changes and run `node --test tests/user-service.test.js` and `node scripts/check-routes.js` to confirm both the tests and the routes work.

